    {
      "id": "grammar-bare-infinitive-diagram-label",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "infinitivo como etiqueta de acción",
      "questionEn": "Why can abrir (“to open”) mean “Open!” on a sign or button?",
      "answerEn": "Spanish can use a verb’s dictionary form to label an action or give a short written instruction. English often translates it without “to.”",
      "noteEn": "The dictionary form is called the infinitive. It ends in -ar, -er, or -ir, as in cerrar (“to close”), comer (“to eat”), and abrir (“to open”). Signs, buttons, and written steps can keep this form unchanged to name what to do. When speaking directly to one person informally, you normally use a different form: abrir becomes abre (“Open!”). The example below is a written instruction.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Cerrar la puerta.",
        "en": "Close the door."
      },
      "tags": [
        "grammar",
        "infinitives",
        "instructions",
        "A2"
      ],
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
      "schedulable": true,
      "provenance": {
        "method": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
